WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.380
I'm Cory Bernatt, and I
run the business called

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.010
First Aid for Leather by
Sandalman in Toronto, Canada.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.803
I'm going to be taking you
through a restoration process

00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:22.053
of a 70-year-old horsehide leather jacket.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.644
I believe the jacket was worth

00:00:26.644 --> 00:00:29.069
about $100 70 years ago,

00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:32.570
and it's probably worth
about $1,500 today.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.620
It would probably be about
a $1,500 restoration.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.930
This is a jacket that was
made out of horsehide,

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.930
which in the past was something
that was quite common.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.890
Nowadays, most leather jackets of its type

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.410
are reproductions made out of cowhide,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.350
which is not as strong as horsehide.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.220
It's basically broken
down on every aspect.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.610
The knit cuffs and waistband.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.455
The leather's completely
deteriorated over time,

00:00:59.455 --> 00:01:02.090
as you can see, the fading and drying out.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.373
The collar, which is made from sheepskin,

00:01:05.373 --> 00:01:07.948
has started to bald and lose hair.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.970
The first thing we have to
do is take everything apart.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.803
Part of the treatment process

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:19.194
is just washing down the
surface of the leather

00:01:19.194 --> 00:01:22.920
with a pH-balanced soap,
which is noninvasive.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.582
It helps to keep the original colors

00:01:26.582 --> 00:01:29.453
without stripping any of the
pigment from the leather.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.270
I'm removing the knit cuff and waistband

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.621
from the jacket by using a seam ripper

00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:39.690
to take it off from the
edge of where it was sewn.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.756
In the case of the knit cuffs,

00:01:41.756 --> 00:01:43.448
it goes around in a circle,

00:01:43.448 --> 00:01:45.973
so you basically just pick
an area, start with that,

00:01:45.973 --> 00:01:48.410
and then just go around
till you get to the end.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:49.743
If we're going with the zipper,

00:01:49.743 --> 00:01:51.068
we would go and start at the top

00:01:51.068 --> 00:01:52.550
and then move down to the bottom.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.192
We do this along all the edges

00:01:55.192 --> 00:01:57.217
where the material is
attached to the leather.

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.453
Then the lining had to be disassembled.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.763
Once the lining is disassembled,

00:02:04.763 --> 00:02:07.011
then we have to break it into its parts.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.607
Once we've disassembled the entire jacket,

00:02:13.607 --> 00:02:15.498
we have to then repattern

00:02:15.498 --> 00:02:17.320
by using the original design,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:18.740
because we have to make
sure that everything

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.232
fits back together in exactly the same way

00:02:21.232 --> 00:02:23.070
as it was made in the first place.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.350
The original collar was
made out of sheepskin.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.740
It is starting to show signs
of what I call balding.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.865
After so many years of use,

00:02:30.865 --> 00:02:33.756
you'll find that the
hair starts to disengage

00:02:33.756 --> 00:02:35.954
from the base material.

00:02:35.954 --> 00:02:37.038
The first thing that we do is

00:02:37.038 --> 00:02:39.622
we disassemble it from
the original collar,

00:02:39.622 --> 00:02:41.696
and then we flatten it
out to allow us to get

00:02:41.696 --> 00:02:44.840
an accurate reading on the measurements.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.018
And then that gets cut out,

00:02:46.018 --> 00:02:47.538
so that way we have all of the proper

00:02:47.538 --> 00:02:50.565
seam allowances to allow
for the stitch to go on.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.632
I've chosen to modernize this design

00:02:55.632 --> 00:02:58.770
rather than trying to restore
it back to its original.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.400
It gives more contrast
to the brown leather

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.780
by using the black trim.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.520
The fabric on the zipper is black,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.120
the knit's in black, as
well as the internal lining

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.690
is made from a quilted
lining made out of black.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.723
So everything has a
consistency amongst the jacket.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.690
Once I take the internal
lining out of the jacket,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.750
we break it into its various parts.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.933
Everything is pressed with an iron

00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:23.806
to make sure that everything lies flat,

00:03:23.806 --> 00:03:25.080
and then we make sure that we have

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.440
enough lining material
when we're cutting it,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.270
because if we don't do this properly

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.070
then it'll end up being short.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.241
When I'm rebuilding a lining for a jacket,

00:03:36.241 --> 00:03:37.250
it has no pockets.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.250
It's just a flat piece of material.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.310
We then have to create the pocket.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.510
In this case, we created
a leather-trimmed pocket.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.210
We have to cut the leather pieces

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.043
that are going to be
incorporated into the pocket,

00:03:49.043 --> 00:03:51.738
and it has to be cut and sewn

00:03:51.738 --> 00:03:53.420
in such a way that when it's completed,

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.010
it is a finished pocket.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:57.770
The cuff is made from a combination

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.490
of cotton and polyester and elastic.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:06.210
So it's woven together so
that it has a nice soft feel

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.360
and at the same time it has
a little bit of elasticity

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.610
so that it holds on your
wrist and on your waistband

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.030
and keeps the air out.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.213
When we're making measurements,

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:17.421
we want to make sure
that we make marks on it

00:04:17.421 --> 00:04:19.543
that are not invasive to the material

00:04:19.543 --> 00:04:21.120
that can easily be wiped off.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.090
I like using a chalkboard char.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.770
It easily comes off.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:26.867
That allows us to get accurate patterning

00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:28.091
and then wipe it off later

00:04:28.091 --> 00:04:29.766
so it doesn't leave a mark.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.068
Once we cut the materials,

00:04:35.068 --> 00:04:37.551
we then have to reassemble everything

00:04:37.551 --> 00:04:40.510
back to the original design.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.900
The toughest part about
reconstructing a jacket like this

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.640
is just making sure that
everything fits together

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.930
exactly as it had begun.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.163
We do this one step at a time

00:04:51.163 --> 00:04:53.640
with each individual aspect of the jacket.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.078
The cuffs have to be
turned inside out and sewn

00:04:57.078 --> 00:04:58.940
and then folded over so that it creates

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.020
the double layer.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.980
All of the linings have
to be sewn back together.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.251
We make notches at various
points of the lining

00:05:06.251 --> 00:05:08.333
so that we know as we're sewing

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:10.591
that we're sewing it to the right point,

00:05:10.591 --> 00:05:13.610
so that where the shoulder
starts and the side begins,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.585
that each of those points
are matched in the right way.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.590
The waistband has to be put in

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.423
before the lining is attached,

00:05:22.423 --> 00:05:24.860
because the lining will
then cover up the knit.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:25.868
The knit gets sandwiched

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:27.851
between the leather and the lining,

00:05:27.851 --> 00:05:29.650
so that way if you were
to turn it inside out

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.575
you'd see a nice clean finish.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.040
I've kind of coined the phrase
"reconditioning leather."

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.341
We do a conditioning treatment

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.358
with the liquid oil for a week,

00:05:43.358 --> 00:05:45.600
and then at the end of that process

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.300
we then move on to the thicker oil.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.575
So we're getting a two-week treatment

00:05:49.575 --> 00:05:50.808
with two different oils

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:53.720
with only a couple of
days' break in between.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.070
After 70 years of use,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.620
all of the initial essential oils

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:02.200
that were part of the
leather have all evaporated.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.008
What I do when I'm treating the leather

00:06:05.008 --> 00:06:07.950
is I'm putting back some
of those essential oils.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.558
You'll notice also the flexibility

00:06:09.558 --> 00:06:10.670
of the leather increases,

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:13.660
because once the oils get
introduced to the leather,

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.120
everything starts to soften up.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.070
All of the fibers inside the leather,

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.554
everything becomes healthier,
darker, more supple.

